You are hive agent "scout-synaptic" (read-only). Read the board rules first: C:[REDACTED:high_entropy].md.
Task: learn how Synaptic-Mesh (C:/Users/mariu/quarantine/ruvnet/Synaptic-Mesh; read only, never run) works. Focus on the neural/mesh core, not docker/k8s/publishing: micro-network nodes (1K-100K params), the DAG substrate, peer coordination and messaging, learning/training rules, memory, agent/task routing, and any SONA/ruv-FANN usage. Map where each feature lives (file paths). Then compare it with OUR neural-mesh (C:/Users/mariu/neural-mesh: read src/*.rs, DESIGN.md, the README if present, RD-LOG.md's FINAL ROUNDUP, and the test names in src/ and tests/). Our mesh.rs Hebbian rule may be the strengthen-only one that hebbian-router's README calls clearly worse; check and report.
Write board/scout-synaptic.md with:
(a) how Synaptic-Mesh works, in 1 page;
(b) a feature-gap table (feature | Synaptic file:line | do we have it? | value for routing, memory or learning from agent outputs | port cost S/M/L | copy or reimplement);
(c) things Synaptic does worse than us, with evidence;
(d) your top 5 recommended ports, ranked, each with a measurable hypothesis that neural-mesh's benchmark could test.
Append a 3-line summary to board/BUS.md. Return a concise report. No edits outside the board.

You are hive agent "router-trainer". Read the board rules first: C:[REDACTED:high_entropy].md. Target: C:/Users/mariu/hebbian-router (Node 24 ESM, no deps; tests: `node --test`; experiment: `node src/experiment.js`; read the whole README, src/, test/ and results/ first). Today's baseline (30 seeds x 2000 tasks): keyword 0.7011, thompson 0.7234, hebbian 0.7239, biomesh-sgd 0.7226. Hebbian ≈ Thompson (+0.0005 ± 0.0072).

Goal: make a learned router that BEATS Thompson and Hebbian under the gate, using the user's router-training brief:
- a linear projection from task features (type + keyword one-hot/bag) to per-agent logits;
- softmax / top-K gating;
- because rewards are bandit feedback (only the chosen agent's outcome is seen) and top-K is non-differentiable: train with a policy gradient (REINFORCE with a baseline) and/or a straight-through or Gumbel-softmax soft-routing estimator; try a contextual-bandit learner too (LinUCB / linear Thompson over the same features) as a strong principled competitor;
- a load-balancing / importance auxiliary loss to prevent collapse onto one agent. Tune its weight, since in this world the optimum is NOT uniform, and report whether it helps or hurts;
- drift handling (decay or a sliding window) for the mid-run skill drift.
Also add a "team" variant (top-K>1 with combined outcome) only if the simulator can express it without changing the existing worlds; otherwise note it.
Method: add the new routers as new files or classes (keep the existing ones byte-for-byte). Add unit tests. Do a hyperparameter search on seeds 1-8 only, disclosed like the README's grid sections. Evaluate on the 30 seeds and confirm on held-out seeds 31-60 (add a flag; do not change the default run). Report paired per-seed comparisons vs thompson and hebbian (mean ± 95% CI, seed wins) overall and per drift window.
Gate: commit to the current branch (local, no push) ONLY the routers that pass the gate, plus README results sections. Report non-winners as honest negatives in the README, and either keep their code clearly labelled or drop it (your call; explain). All 42 existing tests must still pass. Write board/router-trainer.md (what you tried, tables, verdict, and which ideas transfer to neural-mesh's Rust router), append 3 lines to board/BUS.md, and check BUS.md for notes addressed to you before finishing. Return a concise report.

You are hive agent "vector-lab" (a ruflo vector-engineer) in a hybrid hive. Read: C:[REDACTED:high_entropy].md, LLM-DESIGN.md, BUS.md (all of it, especially the queen's vector directive), cartographer.md and router-trainer.md.
Peers you can coordinate with via BUS.md: ingestor (data pipeline), llm-smith (local LLM), mesh-coder (neural-mesh router), ecosystem-scout, sona-trainer. Post on BUS.md before touching anything shared.
Task: decide what ruvector (npm, NOT quarantined, so you may install and run it) can add to C:/Users/mariu/neural-mesh and C:/Users/mariu/hebbian-router. Look at:
(1) its vector database / HNSW / RVF containers (lineage, derive, compact) as the mesh's memory store, or alongside it. Can ruvector's memory become the mesh's memory? Compare it with neural-mesh's own store, which already has int8/PCA192, HNSW and a tested sync, on recall@k, latency, size and durability;
(2) GNN layers / differentiable search;
(3) attention mechanisms (MoE, hyperbolic, local-global) as routing or retrieval features;
(4) hooks: error-record/suggest (error→fix pairs), coedit, trajectories, rag-context, as sources of learning signals from agent work.
Use a scratch install under C:[REDACTED:high_entropy] (ruvector 0.2.25 is installed there). Known bug: `embed` fails (onnx/pkg lacks files, and the add-on is CommonJS), so get 384-d MiniLM vectors from neural-mesh's Rust encoder instead. Also check for a newer ruvector release that fixes embed.
For each candidate, follow the hive gate: hypothesis → measured comparison on the repo's own benchmark (neural-mesh: py/b1 battery for retrieval/memory, src/bin/experiment.rs for routing; hebbian-router: `node src/experiment.js` plus `--heldout`) → KEEP only on a significant paired win with no regression and all tests green; otherwise revert and log it as a negative. Integration must be opt-in. Commit winners locally by explicit path (neural-mesh: coordinate src/ edits with mesh-coder and llm-smith on BUS.md first; never touch their files).
Return ≤ 30 lines: each candidate → verdict (BREAKS / NO EFFECT / IMPROVES / NEGATIVE), with numbers (n, seeds, CI), and commits. If writing a board file is blocked, put the content in the report.

Queen: thanks. Leave the npx cache alone; the user decides on that. Do continue with what's within permissions:
(a) your v1 retrieval lab bin in neural-mesh: announce on BUS first, then run the 30-seed recall@10 / latency / bytes comparison, including the ruvector 0.3.3 VectorDb and RVF arms from scratchpad/vec2;
(b) the 20-sentence cosine check (0.3.3 embed vs the Rust encoder), using files in the scratchpad;
(c) the force-learn and graph-mincut hooks bugs: read the code, write a diagnosis and an upstream-bug draft, and keep any test patch only in scratchpad/vec*.
Same gate. Commit only winners, by explicit path. If any command is denied, stop that item and report it; don't rephrase it to get around the block.

READ-ONLY research. Do not create, edit or commit any files anywhere; return text only.

Context: scoup.ai is pre-product. It builds detention and accessorial evidence packets plus demand letters for mid-market shippers and small carriers, paid on contingency.

Task: understand, with web research, exactly how detention and accessorial money is won or lost (cite URLs and dates):
1. Mechanics:
   - typical free time (2h), rate structures and caps;
   - where the terms live (rate confirmations, contracts, tariffs, broker-carrier agreements);
   - claim and notice deadlines;
   - who pays (shipper, broker, consignee);
   - why fewer than half of detention invoices get paid (ATRI, OIG/DOT 2024 detention report, FMCSA data).
2. What evidence actually wins a disputed detention or lumper claim:
   - ELD/GPS timestamps, check-in/out logs, BOL in/out times, signed POD, photos, geofence;
   - notification-within-X-hours clauses.
   - Also the common rejection reasons.
3. The broker in the middle: how carriers get stiffed when the shipper pays the broker but the broker doesn't pass detention on. Covers double brokering and payment-term issues.
4. Shipper-side reality:
   - accessorial overbilling, duplicate or invalid detention charges, auditing practices;
   - is there a "defense" product opportunity (shippers disputing unjustified charges) as well as "recovery"?
5. Regulation and timing (2025-2026):
   - any US federal detention rulemaking, FMCSA studies, state laws;
   - EU/Norway waiting-time rules (CMR, the Norwegian vegfraktloven, Mobility Package);
   - e-CMR adoption. Each one could be a tailwind.
6. Market size sanity: the $15.1B detention figure (ATRI 2024), detention per stop, frequency, and the small-carrier share.

Output (≤ 900 words):
- the top 8 pains, ranked by money × frequency, each with its source;
- the evidence that wins, as a checklist;
- 5 product implications we may be missing (e.g. pre-emptive notification, rate-con clause extraction, broker scoring);
- confidence grades.

READ-ONLY audit. Do not create, edit or commit any files; return text only.

Repo: C:/Users/mariu/ventures/freight-recovery. The newest code is on branch feat/four-eyes in the worktree C:/Users/mariu/ventures/freight-recovery-build. The marketing site and brand are on master.

Read:
- README.md, ARCHITECTURE.md, CLAUDE.md
- business/ (business-plan, market-analysis, investor-brief), financial/model.md, marketing/seo-and-growth.md, technical/overview.md, technical/web-platform-security.md, docs/data-sources.md
- the web platform's feature surface: api/src routes, web/src/features (claims, approvals, imports, intelligence, apikeys, dev), and the eval harness

Produce a sharp audit:
1. What the product can actually do today, end to end (inputs → outputs). Name the user roles and the core objects (claim, revision, evidence packet, import batch, recovery intelligence).
2. The stated strategy (wedge, ICP, pricing, GTM, acquirers) and the assumptions it rests on. Flag which assumptions are untested or weak, quoting the docs' own A/B/C grades.
3. Gaps between the strategy and the build (e.g. no ELD/TMS integrations, no email ingest yet, no carrier portal, no payment collection).
4. The assets that are genuinely distinctive and could become a moat:
   - the tamper-evident audit chain;
   - four-eyes approval;
   - the honest-claims discipline;
   - tenant isolation;
   - evidence packets with provenance;
   - eval gates;
   - Recovery Intelligence.
5. Ten concrete product ideas that build on what already exists, each with:
   - what it is;
   - why it would differentiate;
   - its effort (S/M/L);
   - what it depends on.
Rank them by impact on the first design-partner pilot.

≤ 1000 words, citing file paths.

READ-ONLY research. Do not create, edit or commit any files; return text only.

Context: scoup.ai is pre-product. It does AI-assembled detention and accessorial recovery for mid-market shippers and small carriers, paid on contingency. Its assets are evidence packets with provenance, a tamper-evident audit chain, four-eyes approvals, multi-tenancy, eval gates, and "Recovery Intelligence" analytics.

Task: find innovation patterns that could make it stand out, using web research and citing every URL and date.
1. Analogous "money-recovery automation" markets and what made the winners win:
   - chargeback and dispute automation (Chargeflow, Justt, Chargebacks911);
   - healthcare claim-denial management (AKASA, Infinitus, Waystar, denial-prediction);
   - parcel refund services (Refund Retriever, 71lbs, Lojistic);
   - e-commerce Amazon FBA reimbursement (GETIDA, Seller Investigator);
   - tax-credit recovery.
   For each, give: pricing model (contingency %, minimums), proof mechanisms, how they automate evidence, win-rate claims and how they're verified, the network effects/data moats, and how they acquired customers.
2. The freight-specific tech frontier 2025-2026:
   - ELD/telematics APIs (Samsara, Motive, Geotab), visibility APIs (project44, FourKites, Trucker Tools), dock-scheduling APIs, EDI 210/214/990, e-BOL/e-CMR;
   - agentic AI in logistics (voice agents calling brokers/shippers, AI email agents for freight ops: names like Pallet, Vooma, HappyRobot, Augment, Fleetworks);
   - verifiable timestamps (RFC 3161 TSA, C2PA for photos, blockchain claims in freight and why they mostly failed).
3. Novel mechanisms worth considering:
   - predictive detention risk per facility (a crowd-sourced "facility score", like the Trucker Path/DAT facility reviews);
   - pre-emptive notification bots that make claims enforceable;
   - automatic clause extraction from rate confirmations;
   - collections escalation ladders;
   - financing detention receivables (factoring advance);
   - insurance-style guarantees ("we pay you if we fail");
   - outcome-based pricing.
Output (≤ 1000 words):
- the patterns that transfer, ranked by how much they would help a pre-product startup get its first 3 design partners and become defensible;
- the patterns that DON'T transfer, and why;
- grade the evidence A/B/C.

You are hive agent "scorecard". Read C:[REDACTED:high_entropy].md, the round-3 section of BUS.md, and the transcript-parsing code in C:/Users/mariu/neural-mesh/ingest/ (reader.py, extract.py). Reuse it; don't duplicate it.

Goal: the user wants to SEE noticeable changes between sessions. Build a cross-session scorecard and a baseline, now, before the other agents' changes take effect.

1. `python -m ingest scorecard` in neural-mesh.
   - Source: all Claude Code transcripts (~/.claude/projects/**, subagents included).
   - Compute per session and per day:
     - tokens per user turn and per subagent dispatch (input, output, cache read, cache write, from usage fields; note any known under-recording);
     - wall time per user turn and per subagent dispatch (from timestamps);
     - tool calls per turn, and Read/Grep/Glob calls per dispatch (the "searching" overhead);
     - hook overhead where measurable (gaps between tool_use and tool_result vs typical);
     - re-dispatch rate and fix-round rate (reuse the ingest labeler);
     - compaction events;
     - the share of cache reads.
   - Output: data/lifelong/scorecard.jsonl (one row per session) and results/scorecard.md, a trend table.
   - Mark which sessions predate the round-3 changes (baseline = everything up to now) so later sessions show deltas with bootstrap CIs.
2. Run it now for the baseline, and report the headline baseline numbers (medians and p90s).
3. Add it to the nightly. Give the queen the exact line for llm/scripts/run_nightly.ps1; do not edit that file.
4. Optionally produce a small self-contained HTML trend page under results/ (no external scripts). The queen may publish it.
Privacy: aggregate numbers only; no prompt text in outputs; scrub anything textual. Commit by explicit path, local only. Tests for the metric functions. Return ≤ 25 lines with the baseline numbers.
